  "findings": [
    "Every Linux environment beat macOS on the same machine at 2 shards: 78–86 s with the repo on the guest's own disk, and 91 s with it mounted from macOS, against 112 s. That is 18–30% less time.",
    "The tests' own CPU time was about the same everywhere (58–71 s of user time). The difference is in the kernel: macOS spent 43 s there, boxery's Lima VMs 9–12 s. Starting processes is much cheaper on Linux, and the suite starts a lot of them (git, bun, node).",
    "VM size barely mattered. Going from 2 to 8 vCPUs saved 6 s in Lima and 6 s in Docker. A small boxery VM (2 vCPU, 4 GiB) is enough for this suite.",
    "At matched sizes, boxery's Lima VMs were 2–4 s faster than Docker (84/81/78 s against 86/86/80 s). Docker's container layer costs almost nothing at 2 CPUs (11 s of kernel time against Lima's 9 s), but its kernel time grew with the CPUs it was given (11, 16, 18, 30 s), while Lima's stayed at 9–12 s.",
    "Mounting the repo from macOS into Docker cost about 11 s (91.5 s against 80.4 s), and still beat macOS.",
    "Running the bare macOS suite at 1 shard took 154 s; letting it use 2 shards brought it to 112 s. hunk only shards by default on Linux."
  ],
  "caveats": [
    "One machine, two runs per configuration. The 1-shard Lima runs varied by 10 s (138–148 s), and the first was the first run in a new VM.",
    "macOS skips 12 more tests than Linux (55 against 43): session end-to-end tests that only run on Linux. That makes macOS do slightly less work, not more.",
    "macOS and Docker used the patched tree, Lima the clean commit. The patch only changes tests (temporary config dirs, resolved temp paths), and Lima had no user config to leak.",
    "Docker Desktop's VM had 7.65 GiB, so the 4- and 8-CPU containers had less memory than the matching boxery VMs. The 4 GiB boxery VM was within 6 s of the 16 GiB one, so memory doesn't look like the bottleneck.",
    "Every Docker run failed the same 2 tests (an adapter-registration test and a file-watcher test). They fail in milliseconds and don't change the times.",
    "The macOS 2-shard baseline was measured at the end of the day, about 15 hours after the Lima runs. The morning's 2-shard runs (109 and 111 s) agree with it but had leaked-extension failures."
  ],
  "artifacts": {
    "write-up": "README.md",
    "card": "card.svg",
    "dockerfile": "Dockerfile",
    "patch": "hunk-test-isolation.patch"
  }
